// NLL's problem case #4: a reference variable reassigned while a reborrow of
// what it pointed at is still to be used. Reassigning the variable does not
// touch what it pointed at, so the reborrow stays good and the old target is
// written through it.
//
// Verdict: accept; prints 10 and 20.
// Basis: borrow-freezing rule (26 Sep 2026) -- reassigning r itself does not
// conflict with a loan of *r: the referent is still alive (Rust's
// 'supporting prefixes' stop there too).
// Rust: RFC 2094, "Problem case #4: mutating &mut references" (the list walk
// 'list = n' while '&mut list.value' is held) -- NLL accepts, because an
// assignment to a reference does not kill loans of its referent's contents
// beyond it; from the RFC, not run. Translated with '&mut' of locals, which
// are 'uni' sources here as Rust's '&mut' targets are.
// Master d331baab: correct.

mod lifetime_free_nll4_reassign_reborrowed_ref;

import stdio use *;

fn show(label &[]u8, n i64) {
  printStr(label);
  printStr(" = ");
  printInt(n);
  printStr("\n");
}

fn main() {
  mut a = 1i64;
  mut b = 2i64;
  mut p = &mut a;
  imm q = &mut *p;
  p = &mut b;
  *q = 10;
  *p = 20;
  show("old-target", a);
  show("new-target", b);
}
